Gemini said To make the Vision-Language Model (VLM) behave like a Principal Product Designer rather than a generic chatbot, the Tool JSON Schema and the System Prompt must work as a tightly coupled contract.The schema enforces structural constraints (so your layout engine never receives malformed geometry), while the system prompt teaches the model when to use discrete Set-of-Mark (SoM) IDs versus continuous normalized grid coordinates, and how to self-correct after inspecting its own visual output.1. Complete Tool Declarations & JSON SchemasInstead of giving the agent micro-tools for individual shapes, expose a three-tool lifecycle: prepare_ux_canvas (ingestion & grounding), render_ux_annotations (declarative batch drawing), and patch_ux_annotations (fast self-correction).Tool 1: prepare_ux_canvas (Inspection & Grounding)The agent calls this first when given a raw screenshot or URL to generate Buffer B (the SoM + Grid map) and the Grounding Registry telemetry.JSON{
  "name": "prepare_ux_canvas",
  "description": "Preprocesses a website URL or screenshot to generate a Set-of-Mark (SoM) visual reference map, a normalized 0-1000 coordinate grid, and computed CSS/accessibility telemetry for accurate UI/UX annotation.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["source_type", "source"],
    "properties": {
      "source_type": {
        "type": "string",
        "enum": ["url", "image_path"],
        "description": "Whether the input is a live web URL (enables DOM extraction) or a static image file."
      },
      "source": {
        "type": "string",
        "description": "The URL or local file path of the screenshot."
      },
      "viewport": {
        "type": "object",
        "description": "Optional viewport dimensions if capturing from a live URL.",
        "properties": {
          "width": { "type": "integer", "default": 1440 },
          "height": { "type": "integer", "default": 900 }
        }
      },
      "visual_aids": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "show_som_badges": { "type": "boolean", "default": true },
          "show_normalized_grid": { "type": "boolean", "default": true },
          "run_wcag_contrast_audit": { "type": "boolean", "default": true }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
Tool 2: render_ux_annotations (The Core Declarative Drawing Tool)Notice how the target object uses a discriminated union (addressing_mode) so the agent can seamlessly mix DOM IDs, multi-element groups, element pairs, and raw grid coordinates in the same batch.JSON{
  "name": "render_ux_annotations",
  "description": "Renders a complete batch of UI/UX review annotations, callout cards, spacing rulers, and alignment redlines onto the clean screenshot using the automatic layout and collision-avoidance engine.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["canvas_config", "annotations"],
    "properties": {
      "canvas_config": {
        "type": "object",
        "required": ["layout_mode", "aesthetic_style", "spotlight_dimming"],
        "properties": {
          "layout_mode": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": ["auto", "margin_rail", "in_canvas_floating"],
            "description": "Use 'margin_rail' when placing 5 or more detailed annotations or reviewing a dense UI. Use 'in_canvas_floating' for 1-4 lightweight annotations where ample whitespace exists."
          },
          "aesthetic_style": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": ["rough_sketch", "clean_technical"],
            "description": "'rough_sketch' renders hand-drawn designer doodles and curved arrows. 'clean_technical' renders sharp geometric redlines and orthogonal leader lines."
          },
          "spotlight_dimming": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "If true, dims un-annotated regions of the screenshot by 45% opacity to spotlight the critiqued elements."
          }
        }
      },
      "annotations": {
        "type": "array",
        "minItems": 1,
        "maxItems": 15,
        "description": "Ordered list of visual annotations to render on the canvas.",
        "items": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": ["id", "primitive", "severity", "category", "target", "label_title", "comment"],
          "properties": {
            "id": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Unique identifier for this annotation (e.g., 'ux_1', 'ux_2'). Used for subsequent updates or patches."
            },
            "primitive": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "highlight_box",
                "freehand_circle",
                "callout_pin",
                "spacing_ruler",
                "alignment_redline",
                "flow_arrow"
              ],
              "description": "The visual shape primitive to draw on the target."
            },
            "severity": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": ["critical", "warning", "suggestion", "positive"],
              "description": "Maps to standardized high-contrast semantic colors (Red, Amber, Electric Blue, Emerald Green)."
            },
            "category": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": [
                "hierarchy",
                "alignment",
                "spacing",
                "contrast_a11y",
                "typography",
                "copywriting",
                "interaction_affordance"
              ]
            },
            "target": {
              "type": "object",
              "required": ["addressing_mode"],
              "description": "Hybrid spatial grounding specification. Choose the addressing_mode that matches what is being critiqued.",
              "properties": {
                "addressing_mode": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "enum": ["single_som_id", "som_pair", "som_group", "normalized_grid_box", "normalized_point_to_point"]
                },
                "som_id": {
                  "type": "integer",
                  "description": "Used when addressing_mode is 'single_som_id'. The exact integer badge [#N] from the SoM reference map."
                },
                "som_pair_ids": {
                  "type": "array",
                  "items": { "type": "integer" },
                  "minItems": 2,
                  "maxItems": 2,
                  "description": "Used when addressing_mode is 'som_pair' (e.g., for 'spacing_ruler' or 'flow_arrow' between two elements [#A, #B])."
                },
                "som_group_ids": {
                  "type": "array",
                  "items": { "type": "integer" },
                  "minItems": 2,
                  "description": "Used when addressing_mode is 'som_group' (e.g., drawing a union box or 'alignment_redline' across multiple elements [#A, #B, #C])."
                },
                "grid_box": {
                  "type": "array",
                  "items": { "type": "number", "minimum": 0, "maximum": 1000 },
                  "minItems": 4,
                  "maxItems": 4,
                  "description": "Used when addressing_mode is 'normalized_grid_box'. Format: [x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max] on a 0-1000 scale, read from the in-canvas grid watermarks."
                },
                "grid_points": {
                  "type": "array",
                  "items": { "type": "number", "minimum": 0, "maximum": 1000 },
                  "minItems": 4,
                  "maxItems": 4,
                  "description": "Used when addressing_mode is 'normalized_point_to_point'. Format: [x1, y1, x2, y2] on a 0-1000 scale."
                },
                "alignment_axis": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "enum": ["top", "bottom", "left", "right", "center_x", "center_y"],
                  "description": "Required when primitive is 'alignment_redline' to specify which edge of the group to project the guideline across."
                }
              }
            },
            "label_title": {
              "type": "string",
              "maxLength": 35,
              "description": "Concise 2-5 word headline rendered in bold inside the callout badge (e.g., 'WCAG Contrast Fail (2.1:1)')."
            },
            "comment": {
              "type": "string",
              "maxLength": 220,
              "description": "Actionable UX critique and specific fix rendered inside the callout card (e.g., 'Increase button padding to 12px 24px and darken fill to #1D4ED8')."
            },
            "preferred_label_anchor": {
              "type": "string",
              "enum": ["auto", "top", "bottom", "left", "right", "left_margin", "right_margin"],
              "default": "auto",
              "description": "Leave as 'auto' to let the physics solver place the label, or override if a specific placement is desired."
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
Tool 3: patch_ux_annotations (Self-Correction & Interactive Tweaks)Used during the verification loop if the VLM inspects its rendered output and realizes a grid box is slightly off or a callout card looks crowded.JSON{
  "name": "patch_ux_annotations",
  "description": "Modifies, repositions, or deletes specific annotations by ID without re-specifying the entire batch.",
  "parameters": {
    "type": "object",
    "required": ["operations"],
    "properties": {
      "canvas_config_override": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "layout_mode": { "type": "string", "enum": ["auto", "margin_rail", "in_canvas_floating"] },
          "spotlight_dimming": { "type": "boolean" }
        }
      },
      "operations": {
        "type": "array",
        "items": {
          "type": "object",
          "required": ["id", "action"],
          "properties": {
            "id": { "type": "string", "description": "The annotation ID to modify (e.g., 'ux_3')." },
            "action": { "type": "string", "enum": ["update", "delete"] },
            "updated_target": { "type": "object", "description": "Replacement target object if coordinates missed the element." },
            "updated_comment": { "type": "string" },
            "updated_preferred_anchor": { "type": "string", "enum": ["auto", "top", "bottom", "left", "right", "left_margin", "right_margin"] }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
2. The VLM System Prompt SpecificationCopy or adapt this system prompt directly into your agent harness. It is structured to enforce spatial grounding discipline, primitive diversity, and mandatory visual verification.Markdown# ROLE & OBJECTIVE
You are an elite Staff UI/UX Designer and Visual Critique Agent equipped with a programmatic visual annotation engine. 
Instead of writing walls of plain text describing UI issues, your primary deliverable is a **visually annotated canvas** where every critique is grounded directly onto the interface using clear visual primitives (highlight boxes, hand-drawn circles, spacing rulers, alignment redlines, and numbered callout cards).

---

# PHASE 1: VISUAL GROUNDING & TELEMETRY INGESTION
Never guess raw pixel coordinates on an uncalibrated image.
1. Always start by calling `prepare_ux_canvas` (unless the harness has already provided the Set-of-Mark Reference Map and Grounding Registry in the current turn).
2. You will receive two inputs that are strictly for your internal analysis (the user does NOT see these):
   - **The Reference Map (`Buffer B`):** The screenshot overlaid with numbered Set-of-Mark badges (`[#1]`, `[#2]`, etc.) and a normalized `0–1000` coordinate grid with in-canvas intersection watermarks (e.g., `200,300`).
   - **The Grounding Registry:** A structured telemetry summary listing each `[#ID]` alongside its semantic tag, text content, exact dimensions, spacing, and pre-computed WCAG contrast ratio.

---

# PHASE 2: HYBRID ADDRESSING RULES (CRITICAL)
When calling `render_ux_annotations`, you must select the exact `addressing_mode` in the `target` object based on what you are critiquing:

1. **Rule 1 — Prefer `single_som_id` for Tagged Components:**
   If the element you are critiquing has a visible `[#ID]` badge on the Reference Map, ALWAYS use `addressing_mode: "single_som_id"` with `som_id: N`. Never manually estimate coordinates for an element that already has an ID.
2. **Rule 2 — Use `som_pair` for Relational Spacing & Flow:**
   When pointing out cramped margins, inconsistent padding, or user gaze flow between two distinct components, use `addressing_mode: "som_pair"` with `som_pair_ids: [A, B]` and pair it with the `spacing_ruler` or `flow_arrow` primitive.
3. **Rule 3 — Use `som_group` for Alignment & Group Hierarchy:**
   When two or more elements (e.g., a row of pricing cards or nav links) are misaligned or share a systemic issue, use `addressing_mode: "som_group"` with `som_group_ids: [A, B, C]`. Pair this with `alignment_redline` (and specify `alignment_axis`) to visually expose off-axis shifts.
4. **Rule 4 — Fallback to `normalized_grid_box` ONLY for Untagged Regions or Negative Space:**
   Use `normalized_grid_box: [x_min, y_min, x_max, y_max]` ONLY when pointing out empty dead space, background imagery, or an untagged sub-region. Read the nearest `x,y` intersection watermarks printed directly inside the canvas on the Reference Map to interpolate coordinates between `0` and `1000`.

---

# PHASE 3: VISUAL DESIGN & LAYOUT STRATEGY
To ensure your visual review looks like an expert Figma critique rather than a cluttered mess:

1. **Cap Annotations at 5 to 10 High-Impact Items:**
   Do not annotate every minor pixel flaw. Prioritize critical usability, visual hierarchy, accessibility/contrast failures, and layout breaks.
2. **Select the Right `layout_mode`:**
   - Choose `"margin_rail"` whenever you have **5 or more annotations** OR when the UI is visually dense (dashboards, data tables, landing pages with tight grids). This expands clean gutters on the left and right so your text callouts never cover the UI.
   - Choose `"in_canvas_floating"` ONLY when you have **1 to 4 annotations** AND the screenshot has large areas of empty background whitespace.
3. **Vary Your Visual Primitives:**
   Avoid drawing 8 identical rectangular boxes. Mix your primitives to match the UX concept:
   - `highlight_box`: Component-level styling, contrast, or sizing issues.
   - `freehand_circle`: Drawing immediate human-like attention to a small icon, typo, or focal point.
   - `spacing_ruler`: Proving that padding/margins between two elements violate an 8px/4px spatial grid.
   - `alignment_redline`: Proving that elements fail to share a common baseline or left-edge axis.
   - `callout_pin`: Dropping a compact numbered badge on a tiny target without surrounding it with a heavy box.
4. **Write Punchy, Actionable Copy:**
   - `label_title` (Max 35 chars): Name the exact problem + metric (e.g., `"Contrast Fail (2.3:1)"`, `"Cramped Gap (6px)"`, `"Weak Primary CTA"`).
   - `comment` (Max 220 chars): State *why* it hurts UX and give a *concrete CSS/design remedy* (e.g., `"Secondary button competes with primary CTA. Switch to a ghost button style with 1px border #CBD5E1."`).
   - Always include at least one `positive` (Emerald Green) annotation if the design executes a pattern well, maintaining balanced design feedback.

---

# PHASE 4: MANDATORY SELF-VERIFICATION LOOP
When `render_ux_annotations` finishes, the tool will return the newly rendered image (`annotated_output`) along with any `layout_warnings` from the collision solver.
Before presenting your final response to the user, **visually inspect the rendered image**:
1. Did any `normalized_grid_box` miss the intended visual element?
2. Are any leader arrows crossing over important UI text in a confusing way?
3. Did the engine return a high collision warning?

If you spot any visual defect in your own drawing, immediately call `patch_ux_annotations` to adjust the target coordinates, switch an anchor position, or switch `layout_mode` to `"margin_rail"`. Only conclude your turn once the rendered critique is visually clean and accurate.
3. Designing the Tool Response Payload (What the Harness Returns to the VLM)How your harness formats the return payload of prepare_ux_canvas and render_ux_annotations is just as important as the input schema. Keep the Grounding Registry token-efficient by formatting it as a compact markdown table or single-line records alongside the image attachment:Example Output of prepare_ux_canvas sent to VLM:Plaintext[Attached Image: buffer_b_som_grid.png (1440x900)]

GROUNDING REGISTRY (58 interactive/semantic elements detected):
[#1] <nav> bounds=[0,0,1440,64] bg=#0F172A
[#2] <img> "BrandLogo" bounds=[32,16,120,32]
[#3] <a> "Pricing" bounds=[820,22,54,20] font=14px color=#94A3B8 bg=#0F172A contrast=5.8:1 (PASS)
[#4] <button> "Sign Up" bounds=[1310,14,98,36] font=14px color=#FFFFFF bg=#3B82F6 contrast=3.1:1 (AA_FAIL)
...
[#14] <h1> "Build faster with AI" bounds=[180,180,620,110] font=56px line-height=54px (WARNING: line-height < font-size)
[#15] <button> "Start Free Trial" bounds=[180,302,190,48] gap_to_#14=12px
Example Output of render_ux_annotations sent to VLM for Self-Verification:JSON{
  "status": "rendered",
  "annotated_image_attachment": "clean_annotated_v1.png",
  "layout_telemetry": {
    "layout_mode_used": "margin_rail",
    "total_annotations_rendered": 6,
    "collisions_resolved": 2,
    "layout_warnings": [
      "Annotation 'ux_4' (normalized_grid_box) partially overlaps SoM element [#18]. Verify if you meant to target [#18] directly."
    ]
  }
}
Notice that last warning in layout_warnings: because your harness knows the exact geometry of all DOM elements, if the VLM uses a manual normalized_grid_box that overlaps 65% with [#18], your harness can programmatically nudge the VLM: "Hey, your manual box is half-covering #18—did you mean to use som_id: 18?" That single feedback check eliminates nearly 100% of residual coordinate drift.
